<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.7.3: Understanding Equations with No Solution or Infinitely Many</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00172</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.7.3: Understanding Equations with No Solution or Infinitely Many</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cbbaf39a-7f4e-4c89-8ebf-1ec40366f4a4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Noah is having trouble solving two equations. In each case, he took
        steps that he thought were acceptable but ended up with statements that are clearly not
        true.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Analyze Noah’s work on each equation and the moves he made. Were they
        acceptable moves? Why do you think he ended up with a false equation?</para>
      <para id="para-00003">Discuss your observations with your group and be prepared to share your
        conclusions. If you get stuck, consider solving each equation.</para>
      <para id="para-00004"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00005"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation.</para>
      <para><m:math
          display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            <m:mo>−</m:mo>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00006"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Apply the Commutative Property.</para>
      <para> <m:math
          display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>−</m:mo>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>1</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00007"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
      <para> <m:math
          display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>1</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00008"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Subtract <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> from each side.</para>
        <para><m:math
          display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>1</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>

      <para id="para-00009"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00010"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation.</para>
      <para><m:math
          display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mn>5</m:mn>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>−</m:mo>
            <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>9</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00011"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Apply the Distributive Property.</para>
      <para><m:math
          display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>10</m:mn>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>−</m:mo>
            <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>9</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00012"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Subtract 10 from each side.</para>
      <para><m:math
          display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>−</m:mo>
            <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>−</m:mo>
            <m:mn>1</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00013"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Add 1 to each side.</para>
      <para><m:math
          display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00014">Why did Noah have no solution for either equation?</para>
          <para id="para-00015">Enter your answer:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">Your answer may vary, but here is an example: All the moves that
            Noah made were acceptable, but because he ended up with a false statement, that means
            there is no value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> that makes the first
            equation true. We can see it in the third line: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. There is no number that
            can make the equation true.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00018">Why did Noah arrive at no solution for the equation solved in
            Example 2?</para>
          <para id="para-00019">Enter your answer:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00021">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00022">Example 1: All the moves that Noah made were acceptable, but because
            he ended up with a false statement, that means there is no value of <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> that makes the first
            equation true. We can see it in the third line: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. There is no number that
            can make the equation true.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Example 2: Noah's moves seem acceptable except for the last one. If
            he had subtracted <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> from each side of the
            equation, he would have <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. Instead, he divided both
            sides by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> which would give an
            undefined number if <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mspace width="0.278em" />
                <m:mspace width="0.278em" />
                <m:mi>i</m:mi>
                <m:mi>s</m:mi>
                <m:mspace width="0.278em" />
                <m:mspace width="0.278em" />
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00024">Noah tried to solve a third equation and ended up with a true
            statement, without variables.</para>
          <para id="para-00025"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00026"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00027"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Apply the Distributive Property.</para>
          <para id="para-00028"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00029"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine Like Terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00030"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Subtract 6 from both sides.   </para>
          <para id="para-00032"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00033"><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Subtract <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> from both sides.</para>
          <para id="para-00034"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00035">Since there is not a variable and the statement is true, any value
            of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> would be a solution for
            the original equation. So, this equation has infinitely many solutions.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00036">We cannot divide the number 100 by zero because dividing by zero
              is undefined. Instead, try dividing 100 by 10, then 1, then 0.1, then 0.01. What do
              you notice happens as you divide by smaller numbers?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00037">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00038">10, 100, 1000, 10,000. For example: The quotients are getting very
              large.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00039">Now try dividing the number -100 by 10, by 1, by 0.1, 0.01. What
              is the same and what is different when you compare to question 1?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00041">-10, -100, -1000, -10,000. For example: The absolute values of the
              quotients are the same, but the sign is opposite..</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00042">In middle school, you used tape diagrams to represent division.
              This tape diagram shows that 6÷2=3</para>

            <media alt="Tape diagram, 3 parts, each marked 2.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_09_06_01.png" />
            </media>

            <para id="para-00043">Draw a tape diagram that shows why  <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>÷</m:mi>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:mfrac>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00044">After you have drawn your answer, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button
              to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00045">

              <media alt="A tape diagram showing twelve groups of 1/2">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_09_06_02.png" />
              </media>

            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00046">Try to draw a tape diagram that represents 6÷0. Explain why this
              is so difficult.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00047">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00048">The zero takes up no space, so no matter how many copies we have
              it could never reach 6.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
        <para id="para-00049"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_1_7_3_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>    </note>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Equations Without Solutions</title>
        <para id="para-00050">What happens when we solve <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>z</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>z</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>?</para>
        <para id="para-00051"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Subtract <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>z</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> to get the <m:math
            display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>z</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> terms together on one side.</para>
        <para id="para-00052"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>z</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>z</m:mi>
                <m:mi />
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>z</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>z</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00053"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Simplify and the <m:math
            display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>z</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> terms are gone!.</para>
        <para id="para-00054"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mi />
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00055">Since 0 does not equal <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, this equation led to a false
          statement. There are no solutions to this equation.</para>
        <para id="para-00056"><emphasis>Example:</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00057">Solve <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>9</m:mn>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>7</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>11</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00058"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as
          much as possible. Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses. Combine like
          terms.</para>
          <para> <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>27</m:mn>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>9</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>22</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
          <para> <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>27</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>22</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00059"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one
          side of the equation.</para>
          <para>Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
          <para><m:math
            display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>27</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>14</m:mn>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>22</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
          <para><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>27</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>22</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00060"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
        <para> The equation <m:math
            display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>27</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>22</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is NOT a true statement. The
          equation 5m+3(9+3m)=2(7m−11) has no solutions.</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00006">
        <title>Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
        <para id="para-00061">What if an equation results in a true statement without variables?</para>
        <para id="para-00062">The equations <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> are all true statements because
          one side is equal to the other. This means that any value of the variable in the original
          equation would be a solution. When this happens in the final step of an equation, there
          are infinitely many solutions.</para>
        <para id="para-00063"><emphasis>Example:</emphasis> Solve <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00065"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as
          much as possible. Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses. Combine like
          terms.</para>
        <para id="para-00066"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00067"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one
          side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
          <para> <m:math
            display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mi>−</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
          <para><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00068"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00069"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check the solution. We say the solution
          to the equation is all of the real numbers.An equation that is true for any value of the
          variable is called an <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">identity</term>.</para>
        <para id="para-00070">The solution of an identity is all real numbers.</para>
      </section>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Equations with No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions </title>
          <exercise
          id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00071">Solve <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>and
        determine the number of solutions.</para>
            <para id="para-00072">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
        button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00073">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00074">Solve this linear equation using a general strategy:</para>
            <para id="para-00075"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation.</para>
            <para id="para-00076"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00077"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Distribute</para>
            <para id="para-00078"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00079"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on
        one side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
            <para id="para-00080"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi />
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mspace linebreak="newline" />
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi />
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00081"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Check the solution</para>
            <para id="para-00082">This is a true statement so there are infinitely many solutions.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

          <exercise
          id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00083">Solve <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> and
        determine the number of solutions.</para>
            <para id="para-00084">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
        button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00085">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00086">Solve this linear equation using a general strategy:</para>
            <para id="para-00087"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation.</para>
            <para id="para-00088"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00089"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Distribute</para>
            <para id="para-00090"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00091"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on
        one side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
            <para id="para-00092"><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi />
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                    <m:mspace linebreak="newline" />
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi />
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00093"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Check the solution</para>
            <para id="para-00094">The equation <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> is NOT a true
        statement. The equation <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
        has no solutions.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>